@SpringBootTest is the primary annotation for Spring Boot integration tests. When you put it on a test class, it tells Spring Boot to find your main application class — the one annotated with @SpringBootApplication — and start the full application context, just like the app would start in production. All your beans are created, all auto-configurations are applied, and your application.properties or yml files are loaded.

You should use @SpringBootTest when you genuinely need the full context — for example, when you want to test that a complete request flows from the controller through the service to the repository and back. It's the integration test Swiss Army knife.

You should NOT use it for every test. If you're testing a single service class, use Mockito without Spring. If you're only testing a controller's request mapping and response format, use @WebMvcTest instead — it starts only the web layer and is much faster.

@SpringBootTest has a webEnvironment parameter that's important to know: MOCK (the default) sets up a mock servlet context without starting a real HTTP server. You use this with MockMvc. RANDOM_PORT starts a real embedded Tomcat on a random available port — you use this with TestRestTemplate or WebTestClient when you want to make real HTTP calls. DEFINED_PORT uses the port number from your configuration.

The key point is to match your test annotation to what you're actually testing.

@ContextConfiguration is the base Spring Framework test annotation. You use it to tell the test framework which configuration class or XML file to load for the application context. It gives you full manual control but no automatic setup — you specify exactly what gets loaded, nothing more.

@SpringBootTest is Spring Boot's testing annotation, and it does much more. It uses @ContextConfiguration under the hood, but it also activates Spring Boot's auto-configuration mechanism, loads your application.properties, sets up the embedded web server if needed, and searches for your @SpringBootApplication class automatically. Essentially, it gives you the full Spring Boot application startup experience in a test.

In modern Spring Boot projects, you'll almost always use @SpringBootTest or one of the slice annotations (@WebMvcTest, @DataJpaTest) instead of @ContextConfiguration directly. The only time you'd use @ContextConfiguration directly is if you have a specific lightweight context you want to build manually without the Boot auto-configuration overhead, or if you're working with plain Spring MVC without Spring Boot.

As a fresher working on Spring Boot projects, you can think of @SpringBootTest as the Spring Boot version of @ContextConfiguration — smarter, more automatic, and Boot-aware.
